* SL_CURAT
* Screen library: put the terminal's cursor somewhere, and remember where, for the cursor lock.
* Copyright (c) 2026, SD Core for Linux.  This program is free software; you can redistribute
* it and/or modify it under the terms of the GNU General Public License, version 3 or later.
*
* START-HISTORY:
* 18 Oct 26 dm  New (terminal 140 by 42, phase 3).  The widgets used to write DISPLAY @(x, y) themselves; they call this instead,
*               so that $sl_key can tell whether the cursor of a guarded program ($sl_guardmode) is on the screen.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   call $sl_curat(x, y)
*
*     x y    the cell, from 0 as in @(x, y).  The terminal's cursor is moved there when the cell is inside the display (@CRTWIDE by
*            @CRTHIGH); a cell outside it is remembered and the cursor is NOT moved, since a terminal puts a cursor addressed past
*            its edge on the last column or row, which is not where the widget means it to be.
*
*   call $sl_curat('off', '')    the widget hid the cursor (it is not in view, or is a container): the lock has nothing to ask
*   call $sl_curat('on', '')     the cursor is shown again where it was
*
*   Where the cursor was put last is the answer to "where would the user type?" for a library program: $sl_key asks it before it
*   waits for a key, when the program has asked for the lock.  A program that has not (a user's SCREEN OPEN program) is not
*   affected beyond the clamp described above.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine sl_curat(x, y)
$catalog $sl_curat

   common /slcur/ cu.has, cu.x, cu.y, cu.hid

   begin case
      case x = 'off'
         cu.hid = 1
      case x = 'on'
         cu.hid = 0
      case 1
         cu.has = 1
         cu.x = x + 0
         cu.y = y + 0
         cu.hid = 0
         if cu.x >= 0 and cu.y >= 0 and cu.x < @crtwide and cu.y < @crthigh then display @(cu.x, cu.y) :
   end case

   return
end

* END-CODE
